{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>And they shall make for themselves fringes, etc.</b> The entire content of the portion requires explanation, for how can one remember all the commandments of HaShem by gazing at a thread of תְּכֵלֶת? And it appears that one may explain this matter in two ways. The first way is, along the lines of what they said in the Sifrei, which the Yalkut brings in the portion of Ha'azinu (§928): The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Tell Israel — gaze upon the heavens that I created to serve you. Perhaps they changed their measure, or perhaps the wheel of the sun rose from the west? And not only that, but it rejoices to do its task, etc. And so He says regarding the sea (Jeremiah 5:22): ״הַאוֹתִי לֹא תִּירָאוּ וְגוֹ׳ אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי חוֹל גְּבוּל לַיָּם״ — perhaps it changed its measure? And not only that, but it struggles to do so and cannot, as it says (ibid. 5:22): ״וַיִּתְגָּעֲשׁוּ וְלֹא יוּכָלוּ.״ From here is proof that the sea does not change its measure out of fear — for it struggles to do so and is not permitted — while the heavens do not change their measure out of love, as they said: and not only that, but it rejoices to do its task. Now regarding the matter of תְּכֵלֶת, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Menachot 43b): תְּכֵלֶת resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the firmament, and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory. And surely its meaning is that these colors resemble one another in likeness — for תְּכֵלֶת does not resemble the firmament except that each inclines in its color toward that which is like it, and each is deeper than the next. And with this statement the author of the midrash sealed the entire matter, saying: what is the point of gazing at a thread of תְּכֵלֶת so that one would thereby remember all the commandments of HaShem and not depart from the measure even by a hair's breadth? Rather, it is because תְּכֵלֶת resembles the sea, and by gazing at this thread one will recall the sea, which resembles it in color, and it will be as though the sea were constantly before his eyes. Then he will contemplate the conduct of the sea, which does not depart from its measure even by a hair's breadth — and from it he will see and likewise do, by way of a kal va-chomer that the author of the midrash employed when he said 'gaze upon the sea.' For 'gazing' is the language of צִיצִית, which is derived from the expression ״מֵצִיץ מִן הַחֲרַכִּים״ (Song of Songs 2:9) — that is, one will peer and gaze at the sea, which does not change its measure; so too he shall not change, all the more so. And if he transgresses even one of all the commandments of HaShem, behold he departs from the measure that HaShem measured out for him, saying: 'Up to here you may come and no further' — by this it is permitted and by that it is forbidden." "But since from the sea one may learn only to serve HaShem out of fear — for regarding the sea it says: and not only that, but it struggles to do its task, etc., and as it says: ״הַאוֹתִי לֹא תִּירָאוּ נְאֻם ה׳ אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי חוֹל גְּבוּל לַיָּם״ — hear from this that from the sea one learns only fear, for fear is a small matter relative to Moses and relative to all like him. But this is not the ultimate perfection, for one who acts out of love and serves HaShem with joy is greater than one who fears Elohim; and he is one who enjoys the fruit of his labor in Torah, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berachot 8a) — for one who acts with joy out of love takes pleasure in the very toil itself, whereas the one who fears does not take pleasure in the deed itself. Therefore it says: and the sea resembles the firmament — for by always recalling the conduct of the sea, it will be as though the sea were constantly before his eyes, and afterward it will also be as though he constantly gazes at the firmament, for the sea resembles the firmament, and from it he will see and likewise do: just as the firmament does not change its measure and not only that but rejoices to serve, so too shall he serve HaShem with joy out of love. And if the servant of HaShem should say: what advantage does the one who acts out of love have over the one who acts out of fear? — to this it says: and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory, for through this he will recall that out of love comes clinging to the Shechinah, the source-place of the soul. For every fearful person distances himself from that which he fears, whereas the lover always strives to cleave to his beloved — and therefore he will receive his reward. It follows that love is the ultimate fulfillment, for through it one merits to cleave to the Throne of Glory; and by peering and gazing at the firmament he will be reminded of the Throne of Glory through the resemblance of the colors. And since there is here an allusion to the revealed reward of this world, which comes through the merit of fear, and to the hidden reward, which comes through the merit of love — as they said (Berachot 8a) regarding one who enjoys the fruit of his labor: אַשְׁרֶיךָ בָּעוֹלָם הַזֶּה, וְטוֹב לָךְ לָעוֹלָם הַבָּא — therefore this passage ״וְעָשׂוּ לָהֶם צִיצִית״ was stated in the hidden [third-person] voice, and afterward ״וְהָיָה לָכֶם לְצִיצִית״ in the direct, revealed [second-person] voice. And this is a precious interpretation. And this is the reason for eight threads and five knots, a total of thirteen, corresponding to the numerical value of אַהֲבָה, love." "The second way is that all the commandments are likened to a garment, as it says (Ecclesiastes 9:8): ״בְּכָל עֵת יִהְיוּ בְגָדֶיךָ לְבָנִים.״ However, there is a difference between them, for an ordinary garment is woven specifically from many threads, for a single thread is insufficient to cover; but the garment of the soul is not so — rather, even a single thread is sufficient to cover it, as Rabbi Yochanan expounded (in Sanhedrin 101a): ״לִבְלִי חֹק״ — for one who did not keep even a single statute; the implication is that if he kept even a single statute, he escapes Gehennom. And the reason for this is because of the covenant that one commandment draws another commandment after it (Avot 4:2), and if so, immediately when he performed even one commandment, all the commandments were in his power to perform — and even though they did not emerge from potentiality into actuality, nevertheless that which is in potentiality is as though it were done. And this is the secret of the thread of תְּכֵלֶת, which reminds one of all the commandments of HaShem, for through it a complete garment is made for the soul and it is not buried naked — unlike the first man, who had only one commandment in his hand and was stripped of it. And there is yet another single commandment that reminds one of all the commandments, and it is ״וְאָהַבְתָּ לְרֵעֲךָ כָּמוֹךָ״ (Leviticus 19:18), and if so, a single thread can bring a person to the ultimate fulfillment — corresponding to the meaning of the word תְּכֵלֶת — and this is a secret of the secrets of the Torah."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:42.587626485Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}